/-! # Products and geometry of diagonalizable groups

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Examples 12.1--12.2, Proposition 12.3,
Lemma 12.4, Remark 12.5 and Theorem 12.9(a), printed pp.231--234
(blueprint: lem-sfb-diagonalizable-group-geometric-criteria).

The group algebra and its Hopf operations are those of `diagonalizableGroup`.
Products are categorical products of group schemes over Spec k. Integer lattices
and cyclic groups are lifted to the common universe of k and M.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A finitely generated character group gives a finite-type diagonalizable group,
with its product decomposition and its characteristic-dependent geometric criteria.
The two isomorphisms in the decomposition clause record that the isomorphism for M
is induced from the displayed character-group decomposition, by contravariance.
The separate free-group clause includes rank zero.

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Examples 12.1--12.2, Proposition 12.3,
Lemma 12.4, Remark 12.5 and Theorem 12.9(a), printed pp.231--234
(blueprint: lem-sfb-diagonalizable-group-geometric-criteria).

Proof:
1. Choose finitely many group generators m_j. Their basis elements e_(m_j)
   and e_(-m_j) generate k[M] as a k-algebra, giving finite type. The Hopf
   identities hold on e_m: both iterated coproducts give e_m tensor e_m tensor e_m,
   the counits fix e_m, and multiplication with its antipode gives e_0 = 1.
   Use def-weil-finite-type-morphism and def-sc-group-scheme.
2. The basis maps e_(a,b) to e_a tensor e_b and back are inverse Hopf algebra
   maps by lem-weil-pbc-algebra-tensor-pushout-universal-property. Also k[Z]
   is the Laurent algebra, and k[Z/n] is k[t]/(t^n-1), sending e_j to t^j.
   Use lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra and def-sc-gm-ga; iterate
   over the decomposition, with k as the empty tensor product. Applying D to
   the inverse character isomorphism gives the asserted induced isomorphism.
3. Use lem-sbc-finitely-generated-abelian-group-structure. The resulting
   Laurent and cyclic presentation has basis x^v times the bounded powers
   of the cyclic variables, by lem-weil-pbc-monic-quotient-free. The same
   basis calculation identifies K tensor_k k[M] with K[M] for every field
   extension, using the affine fibre product and tensor universal property.
4. If every cyclic order n_i is invertible in k, the presentation is finite.
   For a square-zero ideal J in B, units in B/J lift to units in B. If u lifts
   a cyclic coordinate, put a = u^n-1 in J and replace u by
   u-a/(n*u^(n-1)); the square-zero binomial formula gives n-th power one.
   All coordinates therefore lift, giving formal smoothness and hence
   smoothness by def-sbc-formally-smooth, def-sbc-first-order-thickening,
   def-sbc-locally-finite-presentation and def-sbc-smooth-morphisms.
5. In characteristic p, if p divides n_i, t_i^(n_i/p)-1 is nonzero by the
   basis and has p-th power zero. This contradicts reducedness of a smooth
   scheme, by sbc-lem-smooth-geometrically-reduced. Such a cyclic factor
   has nonzero p-power torsion precisely when p divides its order; free
   factors do not. This proves both smoothness claims.
6. If all finite cyclic orders are p-powers, after every field extension
   the ideal generated by t_i-1 is nilpotent and its quotient is a Laurent
   polynomial domain, by lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-domain. Apply
   lem-pur-all-element-powers-spectrum-homeomorphism and
   lem-zl-spectrum-irreducible-nilradical-prime to get an irreducible,
   hence connected spectrum. In characteristic zero use the same argument
   without finite factors. Connectedness is def-weil-connected-scheme.
7. Conversely pass to an algebraic closure (def-weil-algebraic-closure).
   Write n_i = p^a*d with d prime to p, or d = n_i in characteristic zero.
   The d roots of t^d-1 are distinct since its derivative is nonzero there.
   Their powered linear ideals are pairwise coprime, so
   lem-pc-crt-ideal-quotient-product decomposes the coordinate ring into d
   nonzero factors. For d > 1 a nontrivial idempotent disconnects Spec.
   Thus connectedness requires precisely p-primary torsion, or no torsion
   in characteristic zero. Empty products give Spec k, which satisfies
   the conclusions as well.
-/
theorem diagonalizableGroup_geometric_criteria
    (k M : Type u) [Field k] [AddCommGroup M] [AddGroup.FG M] :
    (LocallyOfFiniteType (diagonalizableGroup k M).X.hom ∧
      QuasiCompact (diagonalizableGroup k M).X.hom) ∧
    (∀ (r s : ℕ) (n : Fin s → ℕ) [∀ i, NeZero (n i)],
      ∀ e : M ≃+ ((Fin r → ULift.{u} ℤ) × (∀ i : Fin s, ULift.{u} (ZMod (n i)))),
      ∃ a : diagonalizableGroup k
          ((Fin r → ULift.{u} ℤ) × (∀ i : Fin s, ULift.{u} (ZMod (n i)))) ≅
          ((∏ᶜ fun _ : Fin r ↦ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative k)) ⨯
            (∏ᶜ fun i : Fin s ↦ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.roots k (n i)))),
        ∃ b : diagonalizableGroup k M ≅
            ((∏ᶜ fun _ : Fin r ↦ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative k)) ⨯
              (∏ᶜ fun i : Fin s ↦ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.roots k (n i)))),
          b.hom = diagonalizableGroupMap k e.symm.toAddMonoidHom ≫ a.hom) ∧
    (∀ r : ℕ, Nonempty (diagonalizableGroup k (Fin r → ULift.{u} ℤ) ≅
      ∏ᶜ fun _ : Fin r ↦ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.multiplicative k))) ∧
    (∀ (p : ℕ) [CharP k p], 0 < p →
      (Smooth (diagonalizableGroup k M).X.hom ↔
        ∀ (m : M) (a : ℕ), (p ^ a) • m = 0 → m = 0) ∧
      (GeometricallyConnected (diagonalizableGroup k M).X.hom ↔
        ∀ m : M, (∃ n : ℕ, 0 < n ∧ n • m = 0) → ∃ a : ℕ, (p ^ a) • m = 0)) ∧
    (∀ [CharZero k], Smooth (diagonalizableGroup k M).X.hom ∧
      (GeometricallyConnected (diagonalizableGroup k M).X.hom ↔
        ∀ (m : M) (n : ℕ), 0 < n → n • m = 0 → m = 0)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
